@database "blender"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: blender.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Blender"
@{h1}Blender

Lets an agent work in a Blender that is running on this machine: read the
scene and single objects, run Python (bpy) inside Blender, take a picture
of the 3D viewport and export the scene or the selected objects as glb,
gltf, fbx, obj, stl, ply, usd, abc or a .blend copy. The plugin talks
directly to the BlenderMCP addon inside Blender over a local socket; it
needs no second process and no Python package of its own.

The plugin also brings @{tt}blender_agent@{utt}, a sub-agent that models
and edits scenes: it changes something, looks at a screenshot, and exports
the result into the workspace.

The plugin has no panel and no hooks.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Getting Blender ready  " link blenderside}
  @{"  The Blender agent  " link agent}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Looking: status, scene, object  " link inspect}
  @{"  Running bpy: execute  " link execute}
  @{"  Pictures: screenshot  " link screenshot}
  @{"  Files: export  " link export}
  @{"  Paths, the socket and what it may reach  " link safety}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node blenderside "Getting Blender ready"
@{h1}Getting Blender ready

The tools work only while Blender is open and its addon is listening.

@{number}Install the BlenderMCP addon once. It ships with the
@{tt}blender-mcp@{utt} package (version 1.9.0 here); its installer puts it
into Blender: @{tt}blender-mcp install-addon@{utt}.
@{number}In Blender, enable the addon @{b}Interface: Blender MCP@{ub}.
@{number}In the 3D viewport press @{b}N@{ub}, open the tab
@{b}BlenderMCP@{ub} and click @{b}Connect to MCP server@{ub}. It listens on
port 9876.

Blender has to stay open while an agent works: when it is closed or its
server is stopped, every tool answers with the instruction above. A
screenshot needs a 3D viewport in the open window.

Then talk to @{tt}blender_agent@{utt} (@{tt}/agent blender_agent@{utt},
see @{" Choosing an agent " link scarabhive/agents}) or give another agent
the tools (@{" Setting it up " link setup}).
@endnode

@node inspect "Looking: status, scene, object"
@{h1}Looking: status, scene, object

The tools are the instance name followed by the operation --
@{tt}blender_status@{utt}, @{tt}blender_scene@{utt},
@{tt}blender_object@{utt} for the shipped instance @{tt}blender@{utt}.
The framework does not check arguments against the schema; what follows
is what the code does.

@{h2}status

No parameters. Asks the addon for its version and command list, then
whether each asset provider (PolyHaven, Sketchfab, Hyper3D, PolyPizza,
Hunyuan3D) is switched on in that Blender -- one call each, at most 10
seconds each; a provider that does not answer counts as off.

@{table}
Field | Meaning
addon_version | e.g. "1.6"
protocol_version | the addon's protocol number
capabilities | the commands the addon offers
asset_providers | provider name to true/false
output_directory | where screenshots and exports go, as an absolute path
@{body}

@{h2}scene

No parameters. Answers @{tt}scene@{utt}: the addon's world snapshot --
scene name, @{tt}object_count@{utt}, @{tt}selected@{utt} names and
@{tt}selected_count@{utt}, @{tt}frame_current@{utt},
@{tt}frame_start@{utt}, @{tt}frame_end@{utt}, @{tt}fps@{utt}, the active
camera, lights, and @{tt}objects@{utt}: per object its name, type,
location, rotation and scale (rounded to three places), visibility,
materials and, for meshes, vertex and polygon counts. The addon itself
lists at most 4000 objects and 1000 selected names (flags
@{tt}objects_truncated@{utt}, @{tt}selected_truncated@{utt}).

@{h2}object

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
name | required; the object name exactly as the scene lists it
@{body}

Answers @{tt}object@{utt}: name, type, location, rotation, scale
(unrounded), visibility, materials, and for a mesh its world bounding box
and vertex, edge and polygon counts. An unknown name is an error from
Blender: "Object not found: <name>".

@{h2}Big answers

An answer above 60,000 characters is cut, and @{tt}truncated@{utt} is
true. The answer stays an object: its longest list (normally
@{tt}objects@{utt}) is halved until it fits, and
@{tt}objects_omitted@{utt} says how many entries were dropped. A scene of
a few hundred objects reaches the limit.
@endnode

@node execute "Running bpy: execute"
@{h1}Running bpy: execute

@{tt}blender_execute@{utt} runs Python inside Blender and returns what the
code printed. Everything without a tool of its own goes this way:
creating objects, modifiers, materials, animation, rendering, selecting
objects before an export.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
code | required; the Python source. @{tt}bpy@{utt} is already imported
@{body}

The answer is @{tt}output@{utt} (the printed text, stripped) and
@{tt}truncated@{utt}; output above 60,000 characters is cut and ends with
"...[truncated at 60000 characters]". A script that raises answers an
error "Code execution error: <message>" -- without a traceback, so short
scripts are easier to repair.

@{code}
blender_execute {"code": "bpy.ops.mesh.primitive_cube_add(size=2)\nprint(bpy.context.active_object.name)"}
-> {"status": "success", "output": "Cube.001", "truncated": false}
@{body}

The call waits up to @{tt}long_timeout@{utt} (600 seconds). Blender runs
the script in its own main thread; while it runs, Blender does not
respond. A timeout or a cancelled agent run ends the call, not the script
-- Blender finishes it anyway. The code runs with all the rights of the
Blender process (see @{" Paths, the socket and what it may reach " link safety}).
@endnode

@node screenshot "Pictures: screenshot"
@{h1}Pictures: screenshot

@{tt}blender_screenshot@{utt} has Blender draw the 3D viewport into a PNG
file in the output directory and returns its path -- not the picture. To
look at it, an agent loads the path with @{tt}media_ops_load@{utt} (see
@{" media_ops " link media_ops/load}).

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
filename | file name or path below the output directory; default viewport.png. Any other suffix is replaced by .png
max_size | longest edge in pixels; default 1000, held to 128-4096
@{body}

Answer: @{tt}path@{utt} (absolute) and @{tt}bytes@{utt}. The same name is
overwritten on the next call.

The file is checked after the call: if it is missing or unchanged since
before the call, the answer is an error (@{tt}MissingOutput@{utt}) even
when Blender said success -- so an agent never looks at an old frame. An
empty file is @{tt}EmptyRender@{utt}. Without a 3D viewport in the window
the addon answers "No 3D viewport found".
@endnode

@node export "Files: export"
@{h1}Files: export

@{tt}blender_export@{utt} writes the scene, or only the selected objects,
to a file in the output directory. It exists because a hand-written export
fails silently: glTF and FBX live in @{tt}bpy.ops.export_scene@{utt}, the
others in @{tt}bpy.ops.wm@{utt}, and "only the selected" is spelled
differently in each -- a wrong keyword exports the whole scene and reports
success.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
format | required; glb, gltf, fbx, obj, stl, ply, usd, abc or blend (upper case is taken)
filename | file name or path below the output directory; default export.<format>
selected_only | true: only the selected objects; default false. Select them first with execute. Anything but true or false (also as text) is refused
@{body}

@{table}
Format | Operator | "Selected only" keyword
glb | export_scene.gltf, export_format GLB | use_selection
gltf | export_scene.gltf, export_format GLTF_SEPARATE (a .gltf plus .bin) | use_selection
fbx | export_scene.fbx | use_selection
obj | wm.obj_export | export_selected_objects
stl | wm.stl_export | export_selected_objects
ply | wm.ply_export | export_selected_objects
usd | wm.usd_export | selected_objects_only
abc | wm.alembic_export | selected
blend | wm.save_as_mainfile, copy=True | none: always the whole file
@{body}

@{tt}blend@{utt} writes a copy: the session in Blender keeps its own file
name, so the user's next save goes where it always went. It ignores
@{tt}selected_only@{utt}.

Answer: @{tt}path@{utt}, @{tt}bytes@{utt}, @{tt}format@{utt}. As with
screenshots, a file that is missing or unchanged after the call is an
error (@{tt}MissingOutput@{utt}). An unknown format answers
@{tt}supported@{utt} with the list. The export waits up to
@{tt}long_timeout@{utt}.

@{code}
blender_export {"format": "glb", "filename": "props/crate.glb", "selected_only": true}
-> {"status": "success", "path": ".../data/workspace/blender/props/crate.glb", "bytes": 48213, "format": "glb"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node safety "Paths, the socket and what it may reach"
@{h1}Paths, the socket and what it may reach

@{h2}Where files go

Blender writes screenshots and exports itself; the plugin only hands it
an absolute path. That path must lie below the output directory
(default @{tt}data/workspace/blender@{utt}). Refused, before Blender is
asked:

@{bullet}@{tt}..@{utt} that leads out, absolute paths outside it, drive letters
(@{tt}C:x@{utt} too);
@{bullet}network and device paths (@{tt}//host/share/...@{utt},
@{tt}//?/...@{utt}, @{tt}//./...@{utt}) -- judged on the text alone,
before anything touches the file system, because merely resolving such a
path makes Windows sign in to that host with your credentials;
@{bullet}the output directory itself (@{tt}.@{utt}): it is no file name.

Subfolders below it are created as needed.

@{h2}The socket

Every call opens one connection to @{tt}host:port@{utt}, sends one JSON
command and reads one JSON answer; nothing is kept between calls. The
addon listens on @{tt}localhost@{utt} and asks for no password: every
program on this machine that reaches port 9876 can run Python in Blender
while its server runs. Click @{b}Disconnect from MCP server@{ub} in the
N-panel when you do not need it.

@{h2}What execute may reach

@{tt}execute@{utt} is not sandboxed. The code runs with the rights of the
Blender process: files anywhere, @{tt}os@{utt}, @{tt}subprocess@{utt},
the network. Only screenshot and export are held to the output directory.
That is why @{tt}blender_agent@{utt} is open to admins only
(@{tt}min_role: admin@{utt}); give @{tt}blender/*@{utt} to another agent
only if you would let it run Python on this machine.

@{h2}A Blender on another machine

@{tt}host@{utt} may point elsewhere, and the scene tools and execute then
work there -- over a plain, unauthenticated TCP connection. Screenshots
and exports are written on @{i}that@{ui} machine, so the check that the
file was written looks at a local path and answers
@{tt}MissingOutput@{utt}, unless the output directory is a folder both
machines see under the same path.

@{h2}Timeouts

@{table}
Calls | Setting | Default
status, scene, object | timeout | 60 seconds
asset provider checks in status | fixed | 10 seconds each
execute, screenshot, export | long_timeout | 600 seconds
@{body}

The limit applies to connecting and to each wait for data, not to the
whole answer. A timeout answers an error of type @{tt}TimeoutError@{utt};
Blender keeps working on the command.
@endnode

@node agent "The Blender agent"
@{h1}The Blender agent

@{tt}blender_agent@{utt} builds and changes 3D scenes in the running
Blender and exports the result. You can chat with it
(@{tt}/agent blender_agent@{utt}), another agent can call it as a tool
(@{tt}blender_agent_execute_task@{utt}), and the @{tt}gamedev@{utt} agent
of the godot plugin starts it as a sub-agent through its sub-agent manager
@{tt}gamedev_sam@{utt}. As a sub-agent it runs in a session of its own, so
the screenshots it looks at stay out of its caller's context; what comes
back is a file path.

Its way of working, from its prompt:

@{bullet}Look, change, look again: the scene before an edit, a screenshot
or a fresh scene query after.
@{bullet}One step at a time instead of one long script.
@{bullet}@{tt}bpy.data@{utt} over @{tt}bpy.ops@{utt} where both exist.
@{bullet}Destroy nothing unasked: no deleting objects it did not create,
no overwriting files it did not write.
@{bullet}Exports only through @{tt}blender_export@{utt}.
@{bullet}If nothing answers, @{tt}blender_status@{utt} once, then ask you
to start the server -- no retry loop.
@{bullet}It reports what it built, the object names, the paths of its
exports, and what a screenshot showed.

Its tools: @{tt}blender/*@{utt}, @{tt}media_ops/*@{utt} (to look at a
screenshot), @{tt}datetime/*@{utt} and, from the
@{tt}multi_turn_agent@{utt} it inherits from,
@{tt}context_engineer/*@{utt}. The context engineer hook is on, since
screenshots and scene dumps are large (see
@{" Context Engineer " link context_engineer/hook}). Models: Gemini Flash,
then Gemini Pro, then Claude Sonnet (profiles @{tt}or-gemini-flash@{utt},
@{tt}or-gemini-pro@{utt}, @{tt}or-claude-sonnet@{utt}); at most 60
steps. Only users with the admin role may use it.

Both the agent and the @{tt}blender@{utt} instance are defined in
@{tt}agents/blender_agent.yaml@{utt} in the plugin folder and are loaded
with the plugin; the prompt is @{tt}agents/prompts/blender_agent.md@{utt}.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Six tools, described in the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. Anything
without its own tool is left to execute, so the schema stays small -- the
upstream bridge offered 28 tools, 23 of them asset shops. For
@{tt}blender_agent@{utt} its two tools read "Model, modify or inspect a 3D
scene in the running Blender and export the result into the workspace"
and "List this agent's tools" (@{tt}self_tool_descriptions@{utt}).

Every error answers @{tt}status: error@{utt}, @{tt}error@{utt} and
@{tt}error_type@{utt}. Texts the model gets include:

@{code}
nothing is listening on 127.0.0.1:9876 (ConnectionRefusedError). Start Blender, open the N-panel in the 3D viewport, tab 'BlenderMCP', and click 'Connect to MCP server'.
Blender accepted the connection but closed it without answering — the addon is loaded but its server is likely stopped.
'name' is required
'code' is required
format 'dae' is not supported
'selected_only' must be true or false
'../x.glb' resolves outside the output directory (<folder>); pass a plain name or a path below it
'//host/share/x.glb' is a network or device path outside the output directory; pass a plain name or a path below it
Blender reported success but did not write <path>
<path> is empty
Object not found: Chair
Code execution error: name 'cube' is not defined
No 3D viewport found
timed out
@{body}

The first two have @{tt}error_type: BlenderNotReachable@{utt}.

@{h2}The status line

The chat's status line ends with, for example:

@{code}
Blender addon 1.6 on 127.0.0.1:9876, 9 commands, assets: polyhaven
Scene 'Scene': 3 objects, 1 selected, frame 1/250
'Cube' is a MESH at [0.0, 0.0, 1.0], 2 materials
bpy ran, 8 chars out: Cube.001
viewport -> viewport.png, 312 KB
selection -> crate.glb (glb), 47 KB
@{body}

A line longer than 140 characters is shortened in the middle, so the end
-- where the reason is -- stays.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are appended; nothing earlier changes, and the prompt cache stays
intact. A scene answer grows with the scene, up to 60,000 characters
(about 15,000 tokens); a screenshot costs only its path until the model
loads it with media_ops, and then what an image costs, on every later
request of the run.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin needs no entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}: the
@{tt}blender@{utt} instance is defined next to the agent in the plugin's
@{tt}agents/blender_agent.yaml@{utt} and loaded with it:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    blender:
      type: blender
      enabled: true
      description: "Locally running Blender: scene, bpy, viewport, export"
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+blender/*"
      - "+media_ops/*"   # to look at a screenshot
@{body}

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
host | 127.0.0.1 | where the addon listens (@{" another machine " link safety})
port | 9876 | the addon's port, as set in its N-panel
timeout | 60 | seconds for status, scene, object
long_timeout | 600 | seconds for execute, screenshot, export
output_directory | the data directory's workspace/blender | where screenshots and exports go; a relative path counts from the folder the server started in
@{body}

The keys are read from the instance entry when the server starts; the
@{tt}config:@{utt} block in @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} only documents them.
A different Blender port or folder is set on that entry, or on a second
instance with @{tt}type: blender@{utt}.

@{h2}Dependencies

None in Python -- the socket speaks JSON, the standard library covers it.
Blender with the BlenderMCP addon (@{" Getting Blender ready " link blenderside}).

The older way, the external @{tt}blender-mcp@{utt} bridge, is still
declared in @{tt}config/mcp_servers.yaml@{utt} and switched off. Switched
on, its tools are @{tt}blender.*@{utt} (with a dot) and do not collide
with this plugin's @{tt}blender/*@{utt}; see
@{" External MCP servers " link scarabhive/mcp}.

@{h2}Data

Only the files in the output directory. The plugin keeps no state
between calls.
@endnode
